    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d604">

                <pb facs="0871" n="861" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg861"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/5–445: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hurley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-05-04T18:00:00+07:00">May 4, 1945—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 4—11:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>723. Many of the recent editorials in the Chungking press commenting on
                    Sino-Russian relations (Embassy’s 719 [<hi rendition="#italic">724</hi>], May 3
                        [<hi rendition="#italic">4</hi>], 6 [<hi rendition="#italic">7</hi>]
                        p.m.<anchor n="26" xml:id="d604-f1"/>) are so similar that they appear to
                    have been written under detailed instructions from the Ministry of Information.
                    This tends to substantiate a report from a reliable source that the Ministry of
                    Information has in fact issued a recent directive to the Chinese press to print
                    nothing derogatory to the USSR.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, repeated to Moscow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hurley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
